WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
In this lesson, we're
going to learn a

2
00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.100
little bit about some
of the pitfalls of

3
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.280
retrieval with vectors.

4
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.880
I want to show you some cases where
simple vector search really isn't

5
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.080
enough to make retrieval work
for your AI application.

6
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.620
Just because things
are semantically close

7
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.400
as vectors under
a particular embedding model, doesn't

8
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.380
always mean you're going
to get good results right

9
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.120
out of the box.

10
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.380
Let's take a look.

11
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.630
First thing we need to
do is just get set up.

12
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.030
Our helper utilities this
time will let us

13
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.470
load up everything we
need to load from Chroma,

14
00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.630
have the right embedding
function ready to go, and we're

15
00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.190
just going to do a
little bit of setup.

16
00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.650
So again, we're going to create the
same embedding function, and we're going

17
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.630
to use our helper
function this time to

18
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.210
load our Chroma collection.

19
00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.470
And we're just going to output the
count to make sure we've got

20
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.310
the right number of
vectors in there.

21
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.450
And again, don't worry about any of these
warnings you might see.

22
00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.290
So yeah, that's the right output. There
are 349 chunks embedded in Chroma.

23
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.990
So one thing that
I personally find useful

24
00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.570
is to visualize the embedding space.
Remember that embeddings

25
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.950
and their vectors are a geometric
data structure and you can reason

26
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.170
about them spatially.

27
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.110
Obviously embeddings are
very high dimensional.

28
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.130
Sentence transformer embeddings
have 348 dimensions like we talked

29
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:12.350
about, but we can project
them down into two

30
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.810
dimensions which humans can
visualize, and this can be

31
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.330
useful for reasoning about
the structure of embedding space.

32
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.230
To do that, we're
going to use something called UMAP. UMAP

33
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.590
is Uniform Manifold Approximation, and
it's an open source library

34
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.950
that you can use exactly for
projecting high dimensional

35
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.750
data down into two dimensions or
three dimensions so

36
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.050
that you can
visualize it. This is

37
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:37.190
a similar technique to something
like PCA or t-SNE, except UMAP explicitly

38
00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.850
tries to preserve
the structure of the data in

39
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.510
terms of the distances between points
as much as it can, unlike

40
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.830
for example, PCA, which just tries
to find the dominant directions and

41
00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.030
project data down
in that way. So we're gonna

42
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.030
import UMAP and we'll grab NumPy. And we'll
grab tqdm.

43
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.390
If you don't know what tqdm is, it's the
little thing that basically shows you

44
00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.350
a percentage bar.

45
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.930
When you have some long running process,
I like to use this so that I

46
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.850
know how long the
iterations are taking and

47
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.250
how much longer I might be waiting.
And we're going to grab all

48
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.330
of the embeddings out
of the Chroma Collection.

49
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.270
And what we're going to do is we're going
to fit a UMAP transform.

50
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.490
So again, UMAP is basically
a model which fits a manifold

51
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.330
to your data to project it
down into two dimensions. We're

52
00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.850
setting the random seed
to 0 here just so that we can

53
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.510
get reproducible results and
we get the same

54
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.930
projection every time. So let's go
ahead and fit that transform.

55
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.790
And again, don't worry about any warnings
you might get here.

56
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.010
Now, in this next step, now that we've
fitted the transform, we're going

57
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.050
to use the transform to
project the embeddings. And we're

58
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.610
going to define a function that
does that.

59
00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.790
We're going to call it project_embeddings.

60
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.270
And it takes, as input,
an array of embeddings.

61
00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.830
And it takes the transform itself.

62
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.870
And we're going to start by
declaring an empty array, empty

63
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.050
numpy array of the
same length of as

64
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.110
our embeddings array but
with dimension 2 because we're just

65
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.870
going to get two-dimensional projections out
and what we're going to do

66
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.470
is we're going to project the
embeddings one by one.

67
00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.430
The reason to do it
one by one is just

68
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.390
so that we get consistent
behavior from. UMAP. The way that

69
00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.790
UMAP does projection
is somewhat sensitive to its inputs

70
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.710
so to ensure that we have
reproducible results we're

71
00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.730
just going to project one at a time
instead of in batches

72
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.530
and then of course we're just
going to return the result

73
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.330
of the function, just the
way that you would expect.

74
00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:23.870
Having defined the function, let's
run it on our data set.

75
00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:26.070
And this will take a minute.

76
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.120
Great.

77
00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.020
So now that process is finished,
let's project the embeddings and actually

78
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.920
take a look at them. So we're going
to grab matplotlib.

79
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.240
And probably most of
you are fairly familiar

80
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.440
with matplotlib by now.

81
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.860
We're going to make a figure. And
we're just going to do a

82
00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.160
scatterplot of the projected
embeddings now.

83
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.340
So you can see
we have projected dataset

84
00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.960
embeddings.
The first element of each

85
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.380
one and the second element
of each one and we're

86
00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.060
going to make them size
10 just because it's visually

87
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.220
pleasing. We're going to
set some other properties

88
00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.420
of our axes and
there we go. And this is

89
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.120
what our dataset looks
like inside Chroma projected

90
00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.680
down to two dimensions
and you can see

91
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.920
that we've preserved some structure.
A little bit more

92
00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.860
advanced visualization would allow
you to sort of

93
00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.020
hover over each of these dots and see
what's actually in there and

94
00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.200
you would see that things with
similar meanings end up next to

95
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.680
each other even in the projection.

96
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.180
Sometimes they're a little bit unusual
structures because a two-dimensional projection

97
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.760
cannot represent all
of the structure of the higher

98
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.600
dimensional space. But as
I said, it is

99
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.700
useful for visualization.

100
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:35.020
And one thing that it's useful for is
to bring your own thinking into

101
00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.620
a more sort of geometric setting
and actually think

102
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.900
about vectors and points, which
is what embedding

103
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.020
space retrieval is
really all about. So

104
00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.880
what evaluating the quality and performance
of a retrieval

105
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.160
system is all about is actually
relevancy and distraction.

106
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.560
So let's take a look at
our original query again, the

107
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.100
one that we used in our RAG example.
What's the total revenue? And we're

108
00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.860
gonna do just the same thing as we
did last time. We're gonna query the

109
00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.680
Chroma Collection using this
query, ask for five results,

110
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.820
and we're gonna include the documents
and the embeddings because we'd like

111
00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.420
to use those embeddings
for visualization.

112
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.260
And so we're gonna grab our
retrieved documents out of the results

113
00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.020
again, and let's print
them out.

114
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.300
And we see again,
the same results as we saw before.

115
00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.120
Retrieval is deterministic in this case,
and we see that

116
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.580
there are several revenue-related
documents, but

117
00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.280
also there are things that
here that are might, you know, might

