00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.180
like potentially costs, things
that are to do with money, but

120
00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.700
not necessarily revenue. So let's take
a look at how this query actually looks

121
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.600
when visualized. So what
we're gonna do is grab the embedding

122
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.540
for our query using
the embedding function, and

123
00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.560
we're gonna grab our
retrieved embeddings as well,

124
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.780
which we get from
our result.

125
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.760
And what we're gonna do is use
our projection function to

126
00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.520
project both of these
down to two dimensions.

127
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.720
And then, now that we've
got the projections, we

128
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.380
can visualize them. And
we can visualize them

129
00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.000
against the projection of
the data set. I'll just copy-paste this in.

130
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.240
But it's, again, a scatterplot of the
data set embeddings of the query

131
00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.940
embedding, and of the retrieved
embedding.

132
00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.260
And we're going to set the query embedding
to be a red X. And we're going

133
00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.000
to see the selected
or retrieved embeddings as

